  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nHigh pressure centered across S. Quebec will keep a cool/light \nnortherly flow in place tonight, with mainly clear skies leading\nto good radiational cooling conditions. Lows will range from \nthe mid 30s in the ADKs into the Glens Falls/Lake George area \nto lower 40s in the Hudson Valley south of Albany. Where the \ngrowing season in still in effect, a Frost Advisory has been \nissued for S. Warren and Washington Counties from 2 AM to 9 AM \nThursday.\n\nAfter a chilly start to the day, temperatures will warm into \nthe 60s across much of the area Thu P.M. due to continued \nsubsidence, a dry air mass in place and sunshine. High level \nclouds will increase late in the day, but should not impede \nwarming. The high temperatures will still be slightly below \nnormal.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nMain concern for the Fri to Sun timeframe is a slow-moving \nocean storm developing in the western Atlantic Ocean, east of \nthe mid Atlantic region, that is expected meander north/west \nnear or along the SE New England to Long Island coastline. While\nthere is high confidence in the development of a significant \ncoastal storm, there remains low confidence in the \ntrack/evolution and resulting rain/wind impacts across eastern \nNY and western New England.\n\nMuch of the 12z guidance has trended farther east with the main\ntrack of the surface cyclone compared to the 00z guidance. \nHowever, there is still considerable spread between the various \nguidance camps (EC, GFS, AI-GFS, AI-ECMWF) and also among the \nindividual ensemble members of each guidance source. Timing of \nany bands of showers rotating westward into our area does look \nmore delayed now, which looks more like Sat than Fri. The Sat \nP.M through Sun timeframe looks like the most likely period for\nat least some rain, especially from around the Hudson Valley \neast. In terms of rainfall amounts, latest NBM probs for > 1.0\" \nin the 48 hr period ending 8 AM Mon is around 25-50% from the \nCapital District east, with only a 10-20% chance of > 2.0\". \nBottom line is the rainfall is not expected to be particularly \nimpactful, especially given several days of dry weather leading \nup to the weekend. Should the storm track shift farther west, \nrainfall amounts would increase so will continue to monitor \ntrends. \n\nNortherly winds will increase on the western periphery of the \ncoastal storm Fri through Sat as the pressure gradient\nstrengthens, with probabilities for > 35 mph peak gusts around \n40-60% from the E. Catskills into the higher terrain of W. MA, \nNW. CT and the Rensselaer Plateau. The probability for more \nimpactful gusts of > 45 mph are much lower though, only around \n5-20% in some of these same areas, so Wind Advisories are not \nanticipated at this time.\n\nThe storm may finally start to pull away on Mon as the system \ngradually weakens, but some sources of guidance have the system\nmoving slower, so some showers could linger into Mon.",
